  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Utah's federal contract obligations are $5.1B in FY2026 to date. The federal government made 7,806 awards in Utah this fiscal year to date, and the average award size is $657K. Obligations are down 1.9% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in Utah and obligated $4B through FY2026 to date. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing is the largest industry in Utah and received $2.9B. NORTHROP GRUMMAN SYSTEMS CORP is the largest vendor in Utah and received $2.9B. Historic context shows obligations rose from $4B in FY21 to a peak of $7.2B in FY24 before falling to $5.2B in FY25 and to $5.1B in FY26 to date. Non-defense buyers with notable obligations include the Department of the Treasury at $420M and the Department of Veterans Affairs at $117M. Industries beyond aerospace receiving material obligations include Nonferrous Metal Rolling at $392M and Engineering Services at $217M. This profile reflects a defense-heavy contracting footprint concentrated in a few large vendors and a few large programs.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest federal buyers in Utah right now?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the biggest buyer and obligated $4B in FY2026 to date. The Department of the Treasury is the second-largest buyer and obligated $420M. The Department of Veterans Affairs and the Department of Agriculture follow, with obligations of $117M and $110M respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries dominate federal contracting in Utah?",
        "body": "NORTHROP GRUMMAN SYSTEMS CORP is the top vendor and received $2.9B in FY2026 to date. Asahi Refining USA Inc and L3 Technologies, Inc. are the next-largest vendors, with $392M and $245M respectively. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing is the top industry and received $2.9B, followed by Nonferrous Metal Rolling at $392M and Engineering Services at $217M."
      }
    ]
  },
